## Propose a change

Describe the household task, the current problem, the affected components and your evidence. Show the proposed states and the narrow-screen behaviour. Identify the official source pattern and explain any adaptation.

For a bug, include a minimal reproduction and the Home Assistant and browser versions. For a new pattern, explain why an existing component is not enough.

## Keep one source of truth

Edit the canonical palette data and generate the outputs. Do not hand-edit only one YAML theme or one documentation example. Add a semantic token when the same role appears across components. Do not add arbitrary per-card colours.

Preserve the user-supplied project identity files and their provenance. A new asset needs an explicit design decision and an updated inventory.

<h3 id="maintain-colour-records">Maintain the colour records</h3>

Colour values live in `docs/data/govuk-brand-colours.json`, and every colour page renders its swatches from it. The theme colours live in `design-system/palettes.json`, and the chart colours and energy roles in `design-system/chart-colours.json`. After changing a value, run `node scripts/generate-themes.mjs` and `python3 docs/scripts/sync-palettes.py` from the repository root, and commit the generated files together. Use `--check` on both to find drift.

A change to the chart colour assignment can change existing series styles, so version it and explain it in the change notes.

The social tiles, illustrations and web and app pictures are generated by `docs/scripts/generate-social-examples.py`, `docs/scripts/generate-illustration-examples.py` and `docs/scripts/generate-web-app-examples.py`. Run them again after a colour change rather than editing a picture by hand, and use `--check` to find pictures that are out of date. The working OpenStreetMap example takes its colours from `docs/javascript/map-examples.mjs`. Run `npm run check:colours` after the docs build.

Record each colour rule check in `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`, with the page or component, the theme and mode, the result and any remaining issue. Check again after a change to a palette, component or renderer.

## Review the whole change

Review writing, keyboard behaviour, state handling, contrast, native compatibility, generated files and documentation. Use the [test checklist](../accessibility/testing.html) and the [deviation register](deviations.html).

The [November 2026 visual review](visual-audit.html) records the border, icon and notification-badge deficiencies found after the initial release, their source evidence and the acceptance checks.

## Find audit work

Use the [work-package checklist](guidance-work-package.html) to see the source review, guide coverage and repeated checks. The [design-system audit](design-system-audit.html) lists confirmed gaps and proposed changes against the design-style and writing rules. The [component review](component-audit.html) proposes clearer canonical families and keeps existing URLs. The [missing-component register](missing-components.html) separates absent design specifications from functionality that Home Assistant already provides.

## Publish the theme roadmap

The [theme roadmap](roadmap.html) shows reviewed household features that are accepted, in the works or delivered. It covers the theme, not documentation changes. Use the [roadmap maintenance guide](maintain-roadmap.html) to prepare an issue and review its public wording.

## Follow the source hierarchy

The explicit requirements of the project owner and this repository's documented local contract govern civica. Official systems provide reusable guidance and source patterns. Their government-specific operational instructions do not become instructions to civica's users or maintainers.

[Sources](sources.html), [licensing](licensing.html) and the [documentation architecture](architecture.html) explain how this system is assembled.
